From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 04:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] query_cache: EDEN_NX2_ZPASS_CUT_LOG counts submissions inside a guest's ZPass
 window, EDEN_NX2_ZPASS_PAUSE_AB compares reset and pause in one run, EDEN_NX2_ZPASS_KEEP_WINDOW
 holds back the two that can wait

Core patch on mig020-core (the v1.219.020 series), off unless one of the three switches is set.
Not built, not run.

Each submission runs Scheduler::EndPendingOperations, which resets the ZPass counter unless
EDEN_NX2_ZPASS_PAUSE=1. A guest that resets the counter, draws and reports then gets only the draws
after the submission, often 0, and the game reads that as "occluded" (Kajcsi, Lichii PR 121: same
binary, reset 15 flashes in 492 frames and 9 in 296, pause 0 in 510 and 0 in 507, FENCE_FLUSH_US=0
also 0; the same PR saw 1 in 339 frames and 1 in 1,356 left with the pause, and PR 215 still saw
flashes after it until its report wait went in). That PR's reason for the fence result is that
deferred submissions land anywhere, through the split's idle hook. With the window at 0 no
submission comes from the idle hook, but these still submit in the draw stream without passing
DeferFlush: every 128 draws, RasterizerVulkan::FlushWork, unless a fence or a frame reset the count
first; every ASTC GPU decode, ASTCDecoderPass::AssembleInto; AccelerateDMA::BufferCopy's list copies;
and each Finish. A fence itself now submits right there, and nobody has counted how many fall inside
a window. Whether any of these explain Kokiri's flashes is not known. Against the decodes: run184
had 9,427 by log 561 s and no hot flash before that, and they are about 0.6% of submissions.
Against FlushWork and the decodes both: they come before the draw's own counter starts, so that
draw's samples survive. This patch measures it.

EDEN_NX2_ZPASS_PAUSE=1 carries the count across, but run185 (with it and QUERY_DMA_SYNC=2) stopped
at log 468 s on a GPU tick with no pipelines in it.

EDEN_NX2_ZPASS_CUT_LOG=1, log only: counts submissions by who asked for them, and those that land
in an open guest window with samples at stake. Each report after such a cut carries a note, until
the guest's next reset. Reports are kept by address. Once a second, up to 32 lines
"ZPASS flip: addr= reports= to zero= after a cut= last site= value bits= cut bits=" name the
addresses that went to 0 at least twice, then "ZPASS cut totals" and "ZPASS cut sites" give counts
since the start. Only the first 0 of a row counts as "to zero": after a game hides an object its
next reports can read 0 for reasons of their own. A 0 can be genuine too, so read "to zero ... after
a cut" against "stayed ... after a cut", which need no clock. "Cut zero" alone proves nothing. With
the pause on, "cut no draw" still counts the reports a reset would have left at 0. A report that
drops without reaching 0 isn't seen, and the game's limit is not known.

EDEN_NX2_ZPASS_PAUSE_AB=<seconds>: submissions reset for <seconds>, then pause for <seconds>, round
and round, and each change is logged. One run at one spot compares the two. The pause phases carry
the pause's risks.

EDEN_NX2_ZPASS_KEEP_WINDOW=1: from the guest's ZPass counter reset to its next report, FlushWork
keeps dispatching without submitting, up to 8 times its draw count, and an ASTC decode leaves its
submission to the next one. Nothing on the CPU waits for that decode, as the note in
prepare-vulkan-port.py says, and Target::wait false already takes this path. Fences, list copies
and Finish are left alone. Use with EDEN_NX2_FENCE_FLUSH_US=0: at 500 the idle hook still cuts.

Off, the only changes are a u32 in QueryBase, four members in SamplesStreamer, one bool in the
scheduler, a thread_local store around seven flushes, one bool store per ZPass reset and report, and
one static check per submission. Replaying prepare-vulkan-port.py's adapt() before and after: no
anchor changes, on T42 or on T42 plus 0233 to 0250.
